But still, I'm pretty sure, especially after a lot of years, a lot of kids, there's not much time that goes into like taking care of the relationship.

29
00:01:46,720 --> 00:01:48,160
And that's exactly what Bear does.

30
00:01:48,480 --> 00:01:56,800
So every day you have questions, quizzes, games to really like, you know, connect with your partner and have like a healthier and happier relationship.

31
00:01:57,520 --> 00:01:59,440
And so why I decided to join?

32
00:02:00,240 --> 00:02:02,240
It was I was in maternity leave.

33
00:02:02,640 --> 00:02:05,440
Actually, it was even before, no, it was during my mont maternity leave.

34
00:02:05,600 --> 00:02:11,040
I had a call with Kevin, one of the co-founder, and because he just wanted to get some advice.

35
00:02:11,200 --> 00:02:13,520
And I gave him some advice and then I checked out the app.

36
00:02:13,760 --> 00:02:14,400
I really loved it.

37
00:02:15,040 --> 00:02:17,840
I use it with my husband and he really, really helped us a lot.

38
00:02:18,560 --> 00:02:21,280
Then I had my baby, didn't think about anything else.

39
00:02:21,520 --> 00:02:28,320
And then back in October, I felt like, okay, I need to go back to my maternity leave and I was really ready to take on a new challenge.

40
00:02:28,640 --> 00:02:32,640
So that's why I started looking around, you know, see what the opportunities are.

41
00:02:33,520 --> 00:02:35,040
But nothing really felt right.

42
00:02:35,600 --> 00:02:36,160
I don't know.

43
00:02:36,240 --> 00:02:38,240
I just couldn't really find something that I really care about.

44
00:02:38,400 --> 00:02:40,560
And I needed to believe in the product to join a company.

45
00:02:41,120 --> 00:02:43,600
And I also wanted to lead the entire marketing department.

46
00:02:44,400 --> 00:02:45,920
And then I was talking with my husband.

47
00:02:46,080 --> 00:02:47,760
I was like, something doesn't feel right.

48
00:02:48,240 --> 00:02:49,600
If it was something like paired.

49
00:02:50,240 --> 00:02:56,880
And literally one day after Kevin text messaged me and said that they were looking for a CMO, if I knew someone.

50
00:02:57,360 --> 00:02:59,440
And I was like, That's me.

51
00:03:00,560 --> 00:03:02,400
Sliding into your DMs of founding out.

52
00:03:04,240 --> 00:03:04,640
Exactly.

53
00:03:04,880 --> 00:03:05,760
So that worked.

54
00:03:06,560 --> 00:03:08,000
And that's how I started to work there.

55
00:03:08,320 --> 00:03:18,400
And I think why I joined, well, as I mentioned, I was really ready to take a step up and start leading the entire marketing department before I was taking care of performance marketing growth.

56
00:03:19,040 --> 00:03:23,760
That is like one part of the game, but I really wanted to have the full overview.

57
00:03:24,560 --> 00:03:28,080
Another thing is that I just love early stage startups.

58
00:03:28,320 --> 00:03:29,040
That's my passion.

59
00:03:29,360 --> 00:03:36,640
And I think it's nice to work in an established business that is like eight, ten years old, but it's so much fun to be there at the beginning.

60
00:03:37,280 --> 00:03:41,120
You're twenty people, you have an idea, boom, like you realize that right away.

61
00:03:41,760 --> 00:03:42,560
It's so much different.

62
00:03:43,040 --> 00:03:44,800
And I don't know, it just felt right.

63
00:03:45,280 --> 00:03:47,680
There's a lot of we're 24 people at the moment.

64
00:03:48,480 --> 00:03:50,000
We were 15 when I joined.

65
00:03:50,240 --> 00:03:55,120
So really with this small team, we are agile, we're fast, we're all professional.

66
00:03:55,840 --> 00:04:01,120
There's also, I think, something that they did right is that they invested a lot in like having senior people at the beginning.

67
00:04:01,360 --> 00:04:09,200
And I think this is such a great move because yes, you spend a bit of money, you know, on hiring, you know, good people, but then everyone knows what' they're doing.

68
00:04:10,160 --> 00:04:11,360
Everyone is super independent.

69
00:04:11,760 --> 00:04:12,480
Just felt amazing.

70
00:04:12,640 --> 00:04:13,360
And also the product.

71
00:04:13,920 --> 00:04:16,880
I mean, it's about love and everyone loves love.

72
00:04:17,760 --> 00:04:25,920
And, you know, I think in the first month I received an email from a customer and I literally started to cry.

73
00:04:26,080 --> 00:04:28,320
She was like, I've been with my husband for fifteen years.

74
00:04:28,720 --> 00:04:31,680
I use your app and I feel more connected than ever.

75
00:04:32,160 --> 00:04:37,600
And she mentioned all those little things that she loved about her husband that she forgot about just made my day.

76
00:04:38,000 --> 00:04:39,440
So yeah, I'm really proud.

77
00:04:39,920 --> 00:04:41,120
Yeah, that's really cool.

78
00:04:41,600 --> 00:04:48,320
Working on something that you're so passionate about makes, uh, makes boring computer stuff all day a little bit easier, right?

79
00:04:49,520 --> 00:04:50,640
Exactly, exactly.

80
00:04:51,040 --> 00:04:55,600
And I mean, to be honest, also what I love about this is similar to Blinkers, if you want.

81
00:04:55,760 --> 00:04:59,200
When I joined Blinkers, it was seven years ago, no one knew about it.

82
00:04:59,360 --> 00:05:01,440
I remember telling people, yeah, I work for Blinkers.

83
00:05:01,600 --> 00:05:02,400
People were like, uh, what?

84
00:05:02,720 --> 00:05:03,040
What is it?

85
00:05:03,680 --> 00:05:06,720
Um, but then right now it's a well established brand.

86
00:05:07,120 --> 00:05:07,840
Everyone knows it.

87
00:05:08,000 --> 00:05:14,000
And especially at the beginning, people were like, oh,, why should I pay for like a five minute, fifteen minute version of books?

88
00:05:14,240 --> 00:05:16,000
And people now are like, yes, I get it.

89
00:05:16,240 --> 00:05:20,960
And I think it's really similar for like, you know, the relationship side right now.

90
00:05:21,120 --> 00:05:24,480
People are like, hmm, why do I have to pay for something like that?

91
00:05:24,720 --> 00:05:25,840
Why do I need to care even?

92
00:05:26,240 --> 00:05:37,680
So I think it's really exciting to be the first app, the first brand in this vertical and to make it famous and successful and more competitors will come, but, you know, we're ready for that.

93
00:05:38,320 --> 00:05:39,360
Yeah, that's amazing.

94
00:05:39,840 --> 00:05:54,480
So one of the things that I know you you've been thinking a lot about at Paird is brand marketing and how different your approach to that is from your blinkest days of doing a lot heavier kind of performance marketing user acquisition.

95
00:05:54,720 --> 00:06:02,000
So I'm curious why as part of Paird you think brand marketing is going to be so key to the long term success of the company?

96
00:06:02,720 --> 00:06:02,960
Of course.

97
00:06:04,320 --> 00:06:09,600
So let's start with the fact that I am more of a tech performance person.

98
00:06:10,160 --> 00:06:11,360
That's where my expertise is.

99
00:06:11,760 --> 00:06:13,760
I started computer science and then I started marketing.

100
00:06:14,480 --> 00:06:17,440
So, you know, performance marketing is my sweet spot.

101
00:06:17,760 --> 00:06:18,160
I love it.

102
00:06:18,320 --> 00:06:22,160
And that's what I did in my previous jobs at Kaya and at Blinkist.

103
00:06:22,480 --> 00:06:23,840
I was the first person joining the team.

104
00:06:24,160 --> 00:06:27,600
I mean, that's one of the things that I think Blinkist is really known for doing well.

105
00:06:28,000 --> 00:06:32,880
Like their performance marketing engine is, is, that's why everyone knows what Blinkist is, right?

106
00:06:33,200 --> 00:06:34,000
Because it's everywhere.

107
00:06:34,640 --> 00:06:35,040
Exactly.

108
00:06:35,280 --> 00:06:35,600
Exactly.

109
00:06:35,760 --> 00:06:36,880
And I mean, it's working, right?

110
00:06:37,200 --> 00:06:37,760
It's amazing.

111
00:06:38,320 --> 00:06:43,120
Um, at the same time, I feel I've seen that especially once you start having.

112
00:06:43,200 --> 00:06:45,040
Having maybe an audience that is a bit broader.

113
00:06:45,200 --> 00:06:48,800
I think Blink is still targeting a little bit of a niche, if you think about it.

114
00:06:49,120 --> 00:06:52,320
While, for example, Pair, I mean, it's targeting everyone that is in a relationship.

115
00:06:53,600 --> 00:06:56,800
I think, you know, having a strong brand is so important.

116
00:06:57,520 --> 00:06:59,440
And, for example, think about the dating apps.

117
00:06:59,600 --> 00:07:02,800
Like there are so many dating apps out there, but Tinder is still the best one.

118
00:07:02,960 --> 00:07:05,440
And it's because they build like a super strong brand.

119
00:07:05,840 --> 00:07:11,680
It's even like it's a it's like a common term, even if I'd never used a dating app, but it's still so well known.

120
00:07:12,320 --> 00:07:13,920
So it makes such a difference.

121
00:07:14,640 --> 00:07:18,400
Also performance marketing, I mean, we all know it can become addictive.

122
00:07:18,720 --> 00:07:20,400
There's only so much that you can grow.

123
00:07:21,040 --> 00:07:23,040
And also, I think it's also easy to replicate.

124
00:07:23,280 --> 00:07:28,720
Like I see now that especially in the educational vertical, there's so many new competitors coming up.

125
00:07:28,960 --> 00:07:30,080
It's so hard to keep up.

126
00:07:30,400 --> 00:07:36,240
And, you know, maybe you were the first one doing these campaigns, but these people come in with like a small team.

127
00:07:36,480 --> 00:07:37,840
They copy what you've done and boom.

128
00:07:38,240 --> 00:07:39,280
They kind of steal everything.

129
00:07:40,640 --> 00:07:52,880
At the same time, also, I think that when you're talking about love, there's so much emotion., you know, about that that I feel that just focusing on performance marketing and what works, it will be just one side of it.

130
00:07:53,600 --> 00:08:07,520
So that's why they decided to take a completely different approach and start from day zero building a strong brand that will be recognized, that will be remembered, and that will make actually our paid acquisition a little bit easier, more sustainable, more long term.

131
00:08:08,400 --> 00:08:18,960
So the first thing I did is to hire someone that knows about brand because, as I mentioned, I'm the really excited about, you know, working more on this.

132
00:08:19,120 --> 00:08:26,560
And I've been kind of like taking care of this, like transition, working with brand awareness to defining brand, our brand book.

133
00:08:27,200 --> 00:08:31,440
But next week, we have a new director brand joining and I'm so excited about that.

134
00:08:31,840 --> 00:08:33,280
So we'll see, we'll see how it goes.

135
00:08:33,840 --> 00:08:39,040
Where does a rubber meet the road for an app and actually thinking about brand marketing?

136
00:08:39,760 --> 00:08:43,280
You know, are you thinking about it as kind of a percentage of spend allocation?

137
00:08:44,080 --> 00:08:46,800
Are you thinking about it in terms of, you know, making sure.

138
00:08:47,360 --> 00:08:56,480
that all the ads kind of contribute to that brand, you know, because one of the things in the industry is that the ugly ads perform super well but might not actually be good for your brand.

139
00:08:57,120 --> 00:09:04,400
So, so you see it as kind of like this all encompassing thing, but then also something that you're directly going to put money into.

140
00:09:04,960 --> 00:09:05,120
Yeah.

141
00:09:05,520 --> 00:09:06,800
I think I see on both sides.

142
00:09:06,960 --> 00:09:08,240
So for me, brand is everything.

143
00:09:08,560 --> 00:09:14,640
Every time that you have a touch point with, you know, a user or potential user or your customer, that that's where brand leaves.

144
00:09:14,960 --> 00:09:21,280
So it needs to be to transpire in every single ads message thing that you put out there.

145
00:09:22,320 --> 00:09:27,040
So for example, having like a strong, you know, brand guideline, using them for your ads and so on.

146
00:09:27,600 --> 00:09:32,240
But at the same time, I also think that if you just do that, that's not enough to build a strong brand.

147
00:09:32,560 --> 00:09:33,760
You need to put budget behind.

148
00:09:34,240 --> 00:09:39,440
And there are some platforms, channels that are better at brand awareness than others.

149
00:09:40,320 --> 00:09:46,800
I'm thinking, for example, about, you know, paid content, Albright and Tabula, that's a platform where I spend a lot of time on.

150
00:09:47,280 --> 00:09:49,040
It's a performance channel and everyone will use it.

151
00:09:49,360 --> 00:09:59,360
as a performance channel, but especially in some markets where the competition is not so high, since you pay for CPC, you have so many impressions that it actually becomes stronger than TV.

152
00:10:00,720 --> 00:10:11,120
So you can still do a lot of awareness campaigns without spending millions on TV, because usually small startups don't have that budget to spend, and TV is like a long term game.

153
00:10:11,600 --> 00:10:19,280
So I think yes, allocating budget, but depending on the stage where you are, the company you're working for, the startup, the product, and so on.

154
00:10:19,920 --> 00:10:23,200
So in my case, I would actually be targeting this on both sides.

155
00:10:23,680 --> 00:10:28,400
And at the end of the day, what I'm willing, what I want to see in terms of results, that's the hard part.

156
00:10:28,560 --> 00:10:29,520
How do you measure that?

157
00:10:29,920 --> 00:10:31,440
It's like I want to have more health.

158
00:10:31,760 --> 00:10:33,680
Yeah, it's the question that everyone asks.

159
00:10:35,120 --> 00:10:38,160
I want to have a healthy share between paid and organic.

160
00:10:38,640 --> 00:10:40,000
That's at the end what I want to see.

161
00:10:40,320 --> 00:10:54,240
And I also want to see the overall my paid acquisition costs instead of becoming like higher and higher, they maybe stay at the same level or they even go down because you know, the more you spend, if you spend a million, if if you spend four million, you can expect your conversion rate to go down.

162
00:10:54,720 --> 00:11:03,920
What if by doing more awareness campaign, I'm able to actually maintain that conversion rate, even when I spend more on performance marketing and at the same time I have a lot of organic coming in.

163
00:11:04,400 --> 00:11:05,200
That is the dream.

164
00:11:06,640 --> 00:11:07,280
How to do it?

165
00:11:07,840 --> 00:11:08,800
Yeah, that's hard.

166
00:11:11,360 --> 00:11:16,160
We need to do a follow up podcast in a year and talk through all the failures and successes.

167
00:11:16,640 --> 00:11:17,360
Brand takes years.

168
00:11:17,680 --> 00:11:18,960
That's the thing with brand, right?

169
00:11:19,280 --> 00:11:26,000
Like I, Blinkist, I would say is an established brand, but that took years of performance marketing.

170
00:11:26,560 --> 00:11:30,160
And I would say they have a really you know Blinkist brand when you think about it, right?

171
00:11:30,320 --> 00:11:33,200
It's clean, it's professional, like it's associated with learning.

172
00:11:33,360 --> 00:11:34,960
There's like some very clean associations.

173
00:11:35,360 --> 00:11:46,320
I see pairs as a very obvious place where that I think you can build a very different association, obviously, but like a very similar kind of, you know, associating the emotions and the feeling for the brand.

174
00:11:46,880 --> 00:11:47,200
Exactly.

175
00:11:47,280 --> 00:11:47,360
Yeah.

176
00:11:47,600 --> 00:11:50,880
As I said, it's really hard to know when you have it.

177
00:11:51,200 --> 00:11:52,320
One question I wanted to ask about.

178
00:11:52,400 --> 00:12:09,840
about and obviously you have a new brand director coming in who will help and stuff like this, but how in terms of like how do you envision setting the brand because it is so there's obviously like tactical important things like like you said it has to probably play to love, right?

179
00:12:10,080 --> 00:12:13,360
It has to be accessible, probably a warmer brand than would be something else.

180
00:12:13,440 --> 00:12:28,560
So I'm just imagining here, but like how do you imagine as a marketer involving founders, involving employees, like how do you how do you envision defining the brand at this stage because you're fairly early, like, uh, Parent's been around for how long?

181
00:12:28,960 --> 00:12:30,480
Um, I mean, one year and a half.

182
00:12:30,880 --> 00:12:37,360
So we are pretty new, you know, and also like, you know, the product changed so much in the past one year and a half.

183
00:12:37,600 --> 00:12:44,320
So I opened it back when I was pregnant and then I opened when my daughter was three months old and it was like, oh my God, that's a completely different app.

184
00:12:44,560 --> 00:12:47,520
And that's that's what happened when you're like an early stage startup.

185
00:12:47,680 --> 00:12:49,840
So I think right now we're still in the exploration phase.

186
00:12:50,080 --> 00:12:52,240
I think the product is exactly what we wanted it to be.

187
00:12:52,720 --> 00:12:55,200
Um, we know that, you know, what we have work works.

188
00:12:55,360 --> 00:12:58,080
And now it's just a matter of like better defining the brand.

189
00:12:58,400 --> 00:12:59,280
How are we going to do it?

190
00:12:59,440 --> 00:13:07,680
So, well, the first thing I did is I organized, um, like a session, a brainstorming session with all the founders and the people that have been there from the beginning.

191
00:13:08,880 --> 00:13:13,920
And what I what I did is just I was looking using Miro and I looked for a template brand.

192
00:13:14,880 --> 00:13:15,440
This sounds good.

193
00:13:15,600 --> 00:13:16,320
Let me use this one.

194
00:13:16,560 --> 00:13:17,920
You're giving away all your secrets.

195
00:13:18,640 --> 00:13:19,440
Yeah, exactly.

196
00:13:20,160 --> 00:13:22,800
And I just ran like a super general learning session about that.

197
00:13:23,040 --> 00:13:29,760
It was so good for me to just get, you know, the baseline, but that's also where I define, okay, I need someone to come in and be involved.

198
00:13:30,240 --> 00:13:33,200
We are really lucky that right now we're 24 people.

199
00:13:33,440 --> 00:13:39,120
So it's not a lot like even if we were to involve everyone, to be honest, it's not so bad.

200
00:13:39,440 --> 00:13:45,600
And also I think the people know what they're responsible for and what they can give input, but it's someone else that will have to decide.

201
00:13:46,000 --> 00:13:49,120
That's what I love about, you know, pair that is really, really fast.

202
00:13:49,680 --> 00:13:58,960
So how I wanted to set it up is that I want the brand person to have a specific budget because I feel that without a budget a brand person, you know, they're they're lost.

203
00:13:59,120 --> 00:13:59,680
What do they do?

204
00:14:01,280 --> 00:14:05,680
But also I want for them not to have too many stakeholders involved.

205
00:14:05,840 --> 00:14:10,400
So it's going to be the founders, myself, some other key people that have been there since the beginning.

206
00:14:10,720 --> 00:14:15,120
And to be honest, the one that will drive the strategy is the customer.

207
00:14:15,520 --> 00:14:37,440
So I saw that recently you had a super interesting episode and I don't know, I really, really loved it because you were talking with, I think, Sean regarding the import.ance of listening to your customers and like how to feed a customer's input to the decision that you make in terms of product, the same you're going to do in terms of brand.

208
00:14:38,320 --> 00:14:42,000
So yeah, I'm really excited to see where this is going to take us.

209
00:14:42,480 --> 00:14:54,320
Yeah, and speaking of understanding your customers, I think it's one kind of undervalued aspect of finding marketing channel fit for your product.

